REPACK CONCURRENTLY
===================

The newest of the commands, and the one whose fixes are still arriving.
All of these revert cleanly, which makes them the cheapest entries to
test.

  6ca631b9901  2026-04-28  REPACK CONCURRENTLY: fix processing of
                           toasted tuples
      Concurrent changes are spilled to disk and replayed; some forms of
      toasted tuple were not reconstructed correctly.
      expect: rewrite_fidelity (toast decorator, toast_md5 check)
      revert: clean, one line in repack.c.
      status: CATCHES, 8 failures in 8 runs at stressval 4, as

                stress assertion failed: N rows whose payload does not
                match its md5

              which is silent corruption rather than an error.  Getting
              there took tuning the payload, and the tuning is the
              interesting part: the reassembly writes a plain four-byte
              varlena header over what may be a compressed one, so the
              value has to be compressed to reach it.  A short payload
              compresses back into the tuple and never goes out of line;
              SET STORAGE EXTERNAL goes out of line but never compresses.
              Large and compressible -- a repeated hash, big enough that
              even at fiftyfold compression it clears the toast
              threshold -- is the shape that reaches it, and 0 in 8 with
              the fix restored.

  3be823486f2  2026-07-03  Fix REPACK CONCURRENTLY for stored generated
                           columns
      The transient table had no pg_attrdef rows, so replaying a
      concurrent insert or update of a generated column failed.
      expect: rewrite_fidelity.  The commit says the entries are needed
              for tuples "concurrently updated or inserted", so the
              generated columns hang off abalance -- which every TPC-B
              transaction updates -- and the load inserts and deletes
              rows above the account range as well.  One of the three is
              wide enough to go out of line, so the replay has to
              compute a value and then toast it; one is virtual, which
              it must leave alone; and generated_defs_intact holds the
              expressions themselves to what they were, which no
              invariant over the data could notice.
      revert: clean.
      status: CATCHES.  6 failures in 8 runs at the default six seconds,
              no higher stressval needed, failing with

                ERROR: no generation expression found for column
                number 7 of table "pg_temp_16391"

              which is the transient table missing the pg_attrdef
              entries this commit copies -- the bug itself rather than
              some downstream symptom.  Restoring the fix: 3 runs clean.

              A note for whoever does the next one.  git revert
              --no-commit stages the revert, so "git checkout <path>"
              afterwards restores the reverted file from the index and
              silently leaves the bug in place.  Use git checkout HEAD
              -- <path>, and check the tree is clean before believing a
              green run.

